[
  {
    "topicTitle": "Trivy Fundamentals",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-1-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding Trivy and Its Role in Application Security",
        "content": "Trivy is an open-source security scanner used to identify vulnerabilities and other security risks in software development environments. It can inspect container images, filesystems, source repositories, dependency packages, Infrastructure as Code files, Kubernetes configuration, and secrets. Its purpose is to help development and operations teams discover security problems before applications reach production. Trivy identifies known vulnerabilities by examining installed operating system packages and application dependencies, then comparing package information with vulnerability intelligence sources. It also detects insecure configuration patterns and accidentally exposed credentials when the corresponding scanners are enabled. In a DevOps workflow, Trivy can run locally during development, inside a CI pipeline after a build, or as part of a scheduled security assessment. For example, a team building a Java application can scan its source dependencies and final Docker image before publishing the image to a registry. Trivy provides findings that engineers can investigate, prioritize, remediate, and verify through another scan. It supports shift-left security by moving security checks closer to the developers who introduce changes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-1-section-2",
        "title": "Security Scanning Capabilities and Supported Target Types",
        "content": "Trivy provides several scanning capabilities that address different parts of the software supply chain. Vulnerability scanning identifies known security weaknesses in operating system packages and application dependencies. Misconfiguration scanning evaluates supported configuration files against security rules, such as unsafe container settings or overly permissive cloud infrastructure definitions. Secret scanning searches files for patterns that may represent passwords, tokens, private keys, or other credentials. License scanning can identify software license information and help organizations understand dependency compliance. Trivy can scan container images from local runtimes, archives, and supported registries, as well as source directories and repositories. It can inspect Kubernetes manifests and related configuration files without requiring those resources to be deployed. It can also generate Software Bill of Materials documents describing software components. Each target type has different evidence and scanning behavior, so engineers must select the correct target and scanner options. For example, scanning a Docker image detects packages inside the image, while scanning the source repository can identify dependency declarations and configuration files that may not be present in the final image."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-1-section-3",
        "title": "How Trivy Discovers Packages and Matches Vulnerabilities",
        "content": "Trivy begins by examining the selected target and identifying files, package databases, dependency manifests, or configuration structures that it understands. When scanning a container image, it can inspect operating system package metadata and application dependency files to discover installed components. A package record usually contains information such as its name, version, ecosystem, and sometimes its distribution or package source. Trivy uses this information to determine which vulnerability records may apply. Vulnerability matching is not simply a comparison of version numbers because security advisories can contain distribution-specific package information, affected version ranges, fixed versions, and backported patches. The scanner uses vulnerability database information and package metadata to evaluate applicable findings. Engineers should understand that an identified vulnerable package does not automatically prove that an application endpoint is exploitable. It indicates that the package matches known vulnerability information and requires investigation. A useful workflow is to review the vulnerability identifier, affected package, installed version, fixed version, severity, and dependency path before deciding how to remediate the finding."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-1-section-4",
        "title": "Understanding Vulnerability Identifiers and Security Advisories",
        "content": "Trivy reports vulnerability identifiers that help engineers trace findings to published security information. CVE identifiers are widely used references for publicly disclosed vulnerabilities, while other ecosystems may provide identifiers from operating system vendors, language package communities, or security advisory databases. A finding can contain a vulnerability identifier, package name, installed version, severity, title, description, references, and a fixed version when one is known. The same underlying security issue may appear in multiple advisory sources, so engineers should avoid treating every identifier as a separate independent defect without checking the advisory relationship. A fixed version is especially useful because it gives the team a concrete upgrade target, although compatibility and vendor patching policies must also be considered. During incident response, record the scanner version, scan date, target digest or commit, database freshness, and relevant finding details. This evidence makes results reproducible and helps security teams distinguish newly introduced vulnerabilities from findings that were already present in an earlier release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-1-section-5",
        "title": "Trivy Scanner Components, Databases, and Execution Flow",
        "content": "A Trivy scan depends on several cooperating elements: the Trivy executable, the selected target, enabled scanners, detection logic, and vulnerability or rule databases where required. The command-line interface accepts the target type, scanner selection, output format, severity filters, and other operational options. Trivy then analyzes the target, extracts relevant package or configuration information, and evaluates that information using its detection capabilities. Vulnerability results depend on vulnerability database content, while misconfiguration checks depend on applicable security rules. The database should be sufficiently current for the assessment to provide useful information about recently disclosed vulnerabilities. In restricted environments, database updates may need to be downloaded through an approved process and distributed to isolated build agents. Teams should monitor database update failures rather than silently accepting stale scan intelligence. A production pipeline should record the Trivy version and database metadata alongside scan results. This helps investigators understand whether two scans differ because the application changed, the scanner changed, or the underlying vulnerability intelligence was updated."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-1-section-6",
        "title": "Using Trivy Through the Command Line",
        "content": "The Trivy command-line interface provides a consistent way to execute security scans in developer workstations, build agents, and automation environments. A common starting point is a filesystem scan using a command such as trivy fs . from the root of a source repository. A container image can be inspected with trivy image followed by an image name and tag or digest. Scanner selection can be controlled so that a team runs vulnerability, misconfiguration, secret, or license checks according to its requirements and installed Trivy version. Severity filters can help create focused reports, but they should not be confused with a remediation policy. Output formats such as table and JSON support different consumers: engineers can read terminal output, while automation can parse structured results. Teams should test commands against representative targets and confirm that the expected scanner types are active. A successful process exit alone does not prove that every required security check ran; pipeline logic must also verify the produced report and interpret findings according to the organization policy."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-1-section-7",
        "title": "Interpreting Scan Results and Prioritizing Findings",
        "content": "Trivy results should be interpreted as security evidence rather than as an instruction to upgrade every package immediately. Start by identifying the target, scan timestamp, scanner version, and enabled scanner types. Then examine each finding for its vulnerability identifier, package, installed version, severity, fixed version, and available references. Severity is an important signal, but it is not a complete risk assessment. A critical vulnerability in a package exposed to untrusted network traffic may require urgent action, while another finding may affect a component that is not reachable in the deployed application. Teams should also consider exploit availability, application exposure, business impact, compensating controls, and vendor guidance. Findings without a known fixed version may require a vendor update, a package replacement, or a documented risk decision. Store reports as build artifacts and compare them across releases to understand whether risk is increasing or decreasing. A useful remediation record includes the owner, affected service, chosen action, due date, verification scan, and any approved exception."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-1-section-8",
        "title": "Integrating Trivy into the DevSecOps Lifecycle",
        "content": "Trivy becomes more effective when security scanning is integrated into the complete software delivery lifecycle instead of being run only before a production release. Developers can scan source files and dependencies locally before creating a pull request. CI pipelines can scan changed repositories, built container images, and deployment configuration after automated tests complete. A registry publishing stage can require a successful image assessment before an image is promoted. Kubernetes deployment workflows can inspect manifests before applying them to a cluster. Scheduled scans can also reassess previously published images because new vulnerabilities may be disclosed after an image was originally built. Each stage should have a clear purpose, target, policy, and responsible owner. For example, a pull request check may provide fast feedback on new issues, while a release gate may evaluate the complete image against stricter production rules. Reports should be retained with commit identifiers and image digests. This approach creates traceability between source changes, build artifacts, security decisions, and deployed workloads."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-1-section-9",
        "title": "Trivy Limitations, False Positives, and Operational Considerations",
        "content": "Trivy is a valuable security scanning tool, but its output must be understood within its technical limitations. A scanner can identify known vulnerabilities only when relevant vulnerability information exists and the target package can be recognized. Newly disclosed vulnerabilities may not appear until database information becomes available. Custom-built packages, unusual dependency layouts, unsupported formats, and incomplete metadata can affect detection accuracy. A finding may also require context to determine whether the vulnerable code is reachable or whether a vendor has backported a security fix. Misconfiguration rules cannot replace architecture reviews, penetration testing, or runtime security controls. Secret scanning can identify likely credentials, but teams must still revoke exposed credentials and investigate possible misuse. Large images and repositories may increase scan time and resource consumption. Organizations should standardize scanner versions, database update procedures, output retention, exception approval, and remediation ownership. Trivy should complement secure coding, dependency management, access control, patch management, and runtime monitoring rather than being treated as a complete security program by itself."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Trivy Installation",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-2-section-1",
        "title": "Choosing an Installation Method for the Operating Environment",
        "content": "Trivy can be installed in several ways, and the correct method depends on the operating system, administrative permissions, network access, and intended usage. Linux teams may use supported distribution packages or official installation instructions. macOS developers can use a supported package manager or an official release binary. Windows users can use a supported package manager or obtain an appropriate release executable from the official project distribution. Containerized execution is another option when teams want to avoid installing the executable directly on a workstation or build agent. Before choosing a method, check the official Trivy documentation for currently supported installation instructions and package names because distribution details can change between releases. Prefer trusted official sources and verify downloaded artifacts according to the project guidance. In enterprise environments, administrators may publish approved packages through an internal repository. Standardizing the installation method makes it easier to control versions, update agents, reproduce pipeline behavior, and investigate differences between developer and CI scan results."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-2-section-2",
        "title": "Installing Trivy on Linux Systems",
        "content": "On Linux, Trivy can be installed using an official package repository, a supported package artifact, or a release binary according to the current project documentation. Administrators should first identify the distribution and architecture using commands such as cat /etc/os-release and uname -m. This avoids installing a package intended for a different operating system or processor architecture. After configuring a trusted source, install the package using the distribution package manager and inspect the package installation result. The executable should then be available through the system PATH. On production build agents, installation should be managed through configuration management, a hardened machine image, or a controlled agent provisioning process instead of an undocumented manual change. Restrict write access to the executable and its installation directory so untrusted pipeline jobs cannot replace the scanner. Record the installed version and package source as part of the agent inventory. If a package repository is unavailable, use an approved offline installation process and verify the artifact before execution."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-2-section-3",
        "title": "Installing Trivy on Windows Workstations and Build Agents",
        "content": "Windows users can install Trivy through a supported package manager or by obtaining the appropriate official Windows release executable. Before installation, confirm whether the machine uses a 64-bit or another supported architecture and ensure that the downloaded artifact matches it. Use an approved source and follow the official project guidance for validating release files. If the executable is installed in a dedicated directory, add that directory to the system or user PATH so PowerShell and other command environments can locate it. Open a new terminal after changing PATH because an existing terminal may retain its previous environment. Run trivy --version to confirm that the executable starts and to identify the installed release. On shared Windows build agents, install the binary in a controlled location and restrict modification permissions. Avoid placing a security scanner in a temporary workspace that is deleted between builds unless the pipeline deliberately provisions it each time. Document the installation method so future upgrades and incident investigations can reproduce the environment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-2-section-4",
        "title": "Running Trivy with Docker Instead of a Host Installation",
        "content": "Trivy can also be executed from a container image, which is useful when a workstation or CI agent should not maintain a separate host installation. The container runtime must be available and the runner must be allowed to pull the approved Trivy image. When scanning a local directory, the directory must be mounted into the scanner container and the command must reference the mounted path rather than an unrelated container path. When scanning a container image, the runtime or registry access method must be configured so Trivy can retrieve the target image. Use an approved, pinned Trivy image version instead of relying on an unchanging latest tag in a reproducible production pipeline. Pay attention to filesystem permissions because files created by a container may have ownership that differs from the host user. Also consider proxy settings, certificate trust, registry credentials, and access to the vulnerability database. Containerized execution improves environment consistency, but it does not automatically solve permission, networking, or supply-chain trust problems."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-2-section-5",
        "title": "Verifying Installation, Version, and Executable Availability",
        "content": "After installing Trivy, verify that the executable is available and that the expected version runs successfully. Execute trivy --version from a new terminal session and review the displayed version information. If the shell reports that the command is not recognized or cannot be found, inspect the PATH configuration and confirm that the executable exists in the intended directory. On Linux, commands such as command -v trivy and ls -l can help locate the binary and inspect its permissions. On Windows PowerShell, Get-Command trivy and Get-Item can help identify the command and file location. Check that the installed architecture is compatible with the operating system and that the executable has permission to run. Next, perform a small test scan against a controlled sample target and confirm that Trivy can initialize its required databases. Record the version used by local developers and CI agents. Version verification prevents confusing differences caused by one machine running an older scanner with another machine using a newer release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-2-section-6",
        "title": "Configuring Network Access, Proxies, and Database Downloads",
        "content": "Trivy may need network access to retrieve vulnerability database information and, depending on the target, download image layers or access remote registries. In corporate environments, outbound connections may pass through an HTTP or HTTPS proxy. Configure proxy environment variables according to the operating system and the organization network policy, and confirm that the scanner can reach the required approved endpoints. TLS inspection appliances may require an approved certificate authority to be trusted by the environment; do not disable certificate verification as a routine workaround. If the build environment has no direct internet access, prepare an approved database update and distribution workflow or use the supported offline operation mode documented for the installed version. Monitor database update timestamps so a successful scan is not mistakenly treated as current when its vulnerability data is stale. Registry authentication should use short-lived or narrowly scoped credentials where possible. Avoid printing proxy passwords, access tokens, or registry secrets in pipeline logs. Network readiness is an important installation validation step, not an optional detail."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-2-section-7",
        "title": "Managing Trivy Versions and Controlled Upgrades",
        "content": "A production DevSecOps environment should manage Trivy versions deliberately. An uncontrolled upgrade can change detection behavior, output fields, database compatibility, or the set of available scanning features. Pin a tested version in CI images, build-agent provisioning scripts, or package management configuration so repeated builds use a predictable scanner. Before upgrading, review release notes, supported database behavior, command-line changes, and any changes that affect report consumers. Test the candidate version against representative repositories and container images, then compare the resulting findings with the existing version. Differences should be investigated rather than automatically interpreted as newly introduced application defects. Maintain an upgrade schedule that includes security updates and a process for urgent fixes. Record the scanner version in the build logs and retain it with the scan report. If an upgrade causes a pipeline failure, use the documented rollback process to restore the previously approved version while investigating the incompatibility. Version control makes security results more reproducible and easier to audit."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-2-section-8",
        "title": "Preparing a Secure and Repeatable Installation for CI Agents",
        "content": "A CI installation should be repeatable, least-privileged, and independent of manual workstation changes. Install Trivy as part of a controlled build-agent image, provisioning script, or pipeline tool setup step. Pin the scanner version and obtain installation artifacts from an approved source. Verify the executable before allowing application jobs to use it. Configure database access, proxy settings, registry permissions, and certificate trust through the CI platform secret and configuration mechanisms rather than embedding credentials in source code. Separate the permission to run scans from the permission to publish or deploy artifacts wherever practical. Restrict write access to the scanner binary and shared database cache. For ephemeral agents, decide whether the database cache should be downloaded for each job or provided through a controlled cache mechanism. Add a smoke test that checks the scanner version and runs a small known scan. Finally, document ownership, update frequency, approved versions, and recovery steps so every team uses a consistent and supportable Trivy installation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Filesystem Scanning",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-1",
        "title": "Filesystem Scanning Fundamentals and Security Objectives",
        "content": "Trivy filesystem scanning examines files and directories on a local machine or within a checked-out source repository. It helps developers discover vulnerable dependencies, exposed secrets, and insecure configuration before building or publishing an application artifact. Unlike container image scanning, filesystem scanning begins with a directory or file tree rather than an image manifest. Trivy identifies supported package manifests, lock files, operating system package information where available, and configuration files. The discovered components are evaluated against vulnerability information, while applicable configuration and secret scanners inspect their respective file types. A typical developer workflow starts from the repository root and runs trivy fs . to inspect the project. Teams should decide whether the scan covers the complete repository or a selected subdirectory. The result should be reviewed with the commit identifier, scanner version, database freshness, and enabled scanner types. Filesystem scanning provides early security feedback, but it does not replace scanning the final container image because the image may contain additional operating system packages and runtime components."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-2",
        "title": "Selecting Repository Paths and Controlling Scan Scope",
        "content": "The directory selected for a filesystem scan determines which files Trivy can inspect and therefore directly affects the findings. Running a scan from the repository root provides broad coverage, but monorepositories may contain multiple applications, generated output, test fixtures, vendored dependencies, and unrelated infrastructure files. A team can scan the complete repository or target a specific application directory when focused assessment is required. For example, a repository containing services/orders, services/payments, and infrastructure can be scanned by service to make ownership and remediation clearer. The scan scope should be documented so a passing result cannot be misinterpreted as proof that unscanned directories are secure. Avoid excluding directories simply to reduce findings. First determine whether the files are generated, irrelevant, or intentionally outside the assessment boundary. Keep source manifests and lock files available because they provide important dependency evidence. In CI, record the exact scan path so reviewers can reproduce the assessment locally."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-3",
        "title": "Discovering Application Manifests and Lock Files",
        "content": "Trivy filesystem scanning can identify supported dependency manifests and lock files to understand which application components are present. A manifest commonly describes requested dependencies, while a lock file records more specific resolved versions and dependency relationships. This distinction matters because the version requested by a developer may differ from the version actually selected during dependency resolution. For example, a Node.js project may contain package.json and package-lock.json, while a Python project may use requirements files or other supported dependency metadata. Java applications may include Maven or Gradle dependency information. Trivy uses supported file formats and package metadata to identify components and compare them with relevant vulnerability records. Teams should commit lock files where their package ecosystem expects reproducible dependency resolution. If a lock file is missing or inconsistent with the manifest, the scan may not represent the exact dependency graph used by the build. Validate dependency resolution and inspect which files Trivy detected when results appear incomplete."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-4",
        "title": "Configuring Filesystem Vulnerability, Secret, and Misconfiguration Scanners",
        "content": "Filesystem scans can combine multiple security checks, but each scanner has a different purpose and detection method. Vulnerability scanning examines supported package information and dependency versions. Secret scanning looks for patterns that may indicate credentials, access tokens, private keys, or other sensitive values committed to files. Misconfiguration scanning evaluates supported configuration files against security rules, such as unsafe infrastructure settings or insecure deployment definitions. Teams should explicitly understand which scanner types are enabled by the command and their installed Trivy version. A vulnerability-only scan does not establish that secrets or configuration problems were checked. Enabling every scanner for every repository may also increase execution time and produce findings requiring different remediation owners. A practical pipeline can run broad security assessments on pull requests and focused scans for faster feedback. Record scanner selection in build logs and report metadata. If a required scanner is disabled or skipped because of an unsupported file type, make that limitation visible rather than presenting the assessment as complete."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-5",
        "title": "Understanding Ignore Rules and Excluded Files",
        "content": "Large repositories often contain generated files, test fixtures, cached content, or directories that should not participate in a particular scan. Trivy provides supported mechanisms for controlling scan scope and ignoring selected findings or paths, but teams must distinguish between excluding files and suppressing reported vulnerabilities. Excluding a path prevents the scanner from evaluating that content, while ignoring a finding changes how a detected issue is presented or acted upon. These decisions have different security consequences. Before excluding a directory, verify whether it contains application code, dependency manifests, deployment configuration, or secrets. Prefer narrow, documented exclusions over broad patterns that could hide future problems. Keep exception ownership and review dates so exclusions do not become permanent without justification. In CI, store the effective command and relevant ignore configuration with the scan report. If a finding disappears after an exclusion change, reviewers should be able to identify the exact rule responsible. Never use ignore files simply to obtain a green pipeline without understanding the accepted risk."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-6",
        "title": "Running Filesystem Scans in Local Development Workflows",
        "content": "Developers can use Trivy filesystem scanning before committing code to identify dependency vulnerabilities, secrets, and supported configuration issues early. A simple starting command is trivy fs . executed from the project directory. The developer should confirm that the intended directory is being scanned and that required scanner types are enabled. When a finding appears, inspect the file path, package name, installed version, vulnerability identifier, severity, and remediation guidance. If the finding relates to a dependency, update the dependency declaration or lock file using the package manager rather than manually editing generated metadata. If a secret is exposed, remove it from the current source and rotate or revoke the credential because deleting the visible string does not invalidate an already exposed token. Local scans should use approved scanner versions and sufficiently current databases. Teams can document common commands in the repository so contributors receive consistent instructions. Local scanning complements CI enforcement, but it should not be the only security control because developers may skip local checks."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-7",
        "title": "Filesystem Scanning in Monorepositories and Multi-Service Projects",
        "content": "Monorepositories may contain many independently deployed services, shared libraries, infrastructure definitions, and build tooling. A single filesystem scan from the repository root can provide broad visibility, but the resulting report may combine findings from components owned by different teams. One strategy is to perform a repository-wide scan for overall visibility and additional service-level scans for ownership-specific reporting. For example, an orders service can be scanned separately from a payments service while shared infrastructure configuration receives its own assessment. The pipeline should avoid accidentally scanning only the first service directory or omitting hidden configuration that affects deployment. Define scan boundaries in version-controlled pipeline configuration and review them when repository structure changes. Retain a mapping between service directories, responsible teams, and deployment artifacts. This approach helps teams connect filesystem findings to the application or infrastructure component that must be fixed and makes remediation tracking more practical."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-8",
        "title": "Interpreting Filesystem Reports and Validating Detection Coverage",
        "content": "A filesystem scan report should be reviewed for both the findings and the evidence that expected files were actually analyzed. Start by checking the target directory, scanner version, database information, enabled scanner types, and scan timestamp. Then inspect detected dependency files, package records, configuration findings, and secret findings. A report with zero vulnerabilities is not automatically proof that the repository contains no vulnerable components. The project may use unsupported manifests, dependencies may be generated during the build, or relevant files may exist outside the selected scan path. Compare detected package information with the package manager dependency tree and build output. For important applications, create a test repository with known vulnerable and safe dependencies to verify expected detection behavior. Review warnings about unsupported files or skipped analysis. Store machine-readable JSON output for later analysis and human-readable output for developers. Coverage validation makes a scan result more meaningful than relying only on the absence of reported findings."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-9",
        "title": "Troubleshooting Filesystem Scanning Performance and Access Problems",
        "content": "Filesystem scans can fail or become unexpectedly slow because of permission restrictions, very large repositories, inaccessible files, network delays, or database initialization problems. Begin by confirming that the target path exists and that the user running Trivy can read the relevant directories. Check whether the scan is traversing large build output folders, caches, or dependency directories outside the intended assessment. Review terminal output for database download failures, unsupported file messages, permission errors, and scanner initialization problems. If the scan is slow, measure time spent discovering files separately from vulnerability database updates and result generation. Narrowing the scan path may help, but exclusions must be reviewed to ensure important manifests and configuration files remain covered. In CI, verify that the workspace is fully checked out before launching the scanner. Capture the command, exit code, logs, and relevant environment details when escalating an issue."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-3-section-10",
        "title": "Building a Repeatable Filesystem Scanning Policy for CI",
        "content": "A repeatable filesystem scanning policy defines which repositories and directories are assessed, which scanner types run, how reports are stored, and how findings affect pipeline decisions. Begin by selecting a consistent Trivy version and ensuring vulnerability information is updated through an approved process. Define the scan root explicitly and document excluded paths with a security justification. Choose output formats that support developer review and automated processing, such as terminal output for logs and JSON for reporting systems. Decide which severity levels trigger warnings or failures, while considering whether the policy evaluates all findings or only newly introduced issues. Preserve reports with the commit SHA, branch, build number, scanner version, and scan timestamp. Assign vulnerability remediation to service owners and provide a process for time-limited exceptions. Test the policy against repositories with known findings before enforcing it broadly. A good CI policy provides consistent evidence and actionable feedback without hiding security gaps or creating unnecessary failures."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Dependency Scanning",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-1",
        "title": "Dependency Scanning Fundamentals and Software Supply Chain Risk",
        "content": "Dependency scanning examines third-party software components used by an application and checks whether their versions are associated with known security vulnerabilities. Modern applications rely on libraries, frameworks, build plugins, and transitive packages maintained by many different organizations. A vulnerability in an indirect dependency can affect an application even when developers never added that package directly. Trivy can inspect supported dependency manifests and lock files to discover package information and match it against vulnerability intelligence. This helps development teams identify security risk before a dependency becomes part of a released artifact. Dependency scanning is an important software supply chain control because application security depends not only on custom source code but also on external components. For example, a Java service may directly depend on a web framework that itself pulls in several transitive libraries. A vulnerable transitive library may require upgrading the framework or applying a compatible dependency constraint. Teams should combine scanner findings with package manager information and vendor advisories before selecting remediation."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-2",
        "title": "Direct Dependencies, Transitive Dependencies, and Dependency Trees",
        "content": "A direct dependency is a package explicitly declared by an application or its build configuration. A transitive dependency is introduced indirectly because another package requires it. This distinction is important during vulnerability remediation because the vulnerable package may not appear in the application's primary dependency declaration. For example, an application may depend directly on a web framework, while that framework depends on a networking library containing a known vulnerability. Trivy can report package information discovered from supported dependency metadata, and the package manager can help explain how the package entered the dependency tree. Engineers should identify the parent dependency path before changing versions. Updating a direct package may automatically replace the vulnerable transitive package, while other cases require a dependency constraint or an upstream framework update. Avoid blindly adding a second version without understanding ecosystem resolution behavior. After remediation, regenerate the lock file, rebuild the application, and scan again to verify that the affected package version is no longer present."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-3",
        "title": "Scanning Dependencies Across Programming Ecosystems",
        "content": "Application repositories use different package managers and dependency formats, so dependency scanning must account for the ecosystem in use. Java projects commonly use Maven or Gradle metadata, JavaScript applications use npm or related package manager files, Python projects use requirements or project dependency metadata, and Go projects use module information. Other ecosystems have their own manifests and lock files. Trivy supports multiple dependency formats, but detection depends on the exact file types and metadata supported by the installed version. Teams should consult current Trivy documentation when introducing a new language or package manager. Confirm that expected manifests and lock files are present in the scanned directory. A repository can contain several languages, so a successful scan of one ecosystem does not guarantee that every dependency format was recognized. Compare discovered packages with the package manager dependency listing and inspect warnings for unsupported files. This validation is especially important in polyglot repositories where services use different build systems."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-4",
        "title": "Lock Files, Reproducible Builds, and Accurate Dependency Versions",
        "content": "Lock files record resolved dependency versions and are essential for reproducing many application builds. A manifest may specify a version range or desired dependency, while the lock file captures the specific versions selected during resolution. Dependency scanning becomes more reliable when the scanner can inspect the same resolved package information that the build uses. If a lock file is missing or outdated, a scan may not represent the exact components included in the compiled application. Teams should generate and update lock files using supported package manager commands, review changes, and commit them according to project policy. Avoid manually editing lock file content because it can break integrity metadata or dependency relationships. In CI, use reproducible installation commands that honor the committed lock file rather than resolving arbitrary new versions during every build. Compare scan findings with the actual build dependency tree when discrepancies appear. Accurate dependency metadata helps engineers identify the package version that must be upgraded and makes security results more consistent across environments."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-5",
        "title": "Understanding Vulnerability Matching for Application Packages",
        "content": "When Trivy discovers an application package, it evaluates package identity and version information against available vulnerability records. Correct matching may depend on package ecosystem, package name, version range, and advisory metadata. Two packages with similar names can belong to different ecosystems or publishers, so engineers should inspect the complete package identity before assuming a finding applies. Vulnerability records may specify affected version intervals and fixed versions, but interpretation can differ between ecosystems. Some distributions and vendors backport security patches without changing the upstream version in the expected way. This is why package source and vendor advisory information can be important during triage. Review the vulnerability identifier, package location, installed version, fixed version, and references before making a change. If the result appears incorrect, collect package metadata and scanner version and compare the finding with authoritative advisory information. Do not dismiss a finding solely because the application works normally; vulnerability matching is a security assessment, not a functional test."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-6",
        "title": "Evaluating Severity, Exploitability, and Business Context",
        "content": "Dependency scan findings often include severity information that helps teams prioritize investigation, but severity should not be treated as the only measure of business risk. A critical vulnerability in a package reachable through an internet-facing service may demand immediate remediation. A high-severity issue in a development-only tool may have a different deployment impact, although build infrastructure can still be a security target. Consider whether vulnerable functionality is present, whether affected code is reachable, whether exploitation is known, and what privileges or data could be exposed. Also evaluate the application's deployment context, compensating controls, and availability of a fixed release. Trivy findings provide a starting point for this assessment, not a complete exploitability determination. Record why a team prioritizes or defers a finding and identify the person responsible for the decision. Risk-based prioritization prevents teams from treating every package finding identically while ensuring serious production exposure receives appropriate attention."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-7",
        "title": "Using Fixed Versions and Safe Dependency Upgrade Strategies",
        "content": "A fixed version reported by a vulnerability scanner is a useful starting point for remediation, but upgrading a dependency requires compatibility testing. First identify whether the vulnerable package is direct or transitive and determine which parent dependency introduces it. Review the advisory and release notes to understand whether the fixed version contains breaking changes or additional migration requirements. Update the dependency through the package manager, regenerate the lock file, and inspect the dependency tree to confirm that the intended version was selected. Run unit tests, integration tests, and relevant application smoke tests before approving the change. If the vulnerable package is transitive, upgrading the parent framework may be safer than forcing an unsupported version of the lower-level library. When no fixed version exists, consider an upstream vendor update, package replacement, mitigation, or formally approved exception. After the change, run Trivy again against the repository and built artifact. A successful remediation requires evidence that the vulnerable component is no longer present or that an accepted mitigation has been implemented."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-8",
        "title": "Detecting Vulnerable Dependencies in CI and Pull Requests",
        "content": "Dependency scanning in a CI pipeline provides repeatable security feedback whenever application code or dependency metadata changes. A pipeline can run Trivy filesystem scanning after source checkout and dependency resolution, then retain a JSON report as a build artifact. Pull request checks can focus attention on newly introduced vulnerabilities so developers understand the security impact of their change. A release pipeline may apply a stricter policy to the complete dependency inventory. The pipeline should record the commit SHA, branch, scanner version, database freshness, and scan command. It should distinguish scanner execution errors from policy failures caused by findings. For example, an unavailable vulnerability database should not be silently reported as a clean scan. Teams can publish concise summaries in pull request checks while retaining the full report for investigation. Define clear ownership for remediation and exception approval. This approach makes dependency risk visible during code review and reduces the chance that a vulnerable package is discovered only after production deployment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-9",
        "title": "Managing Findings, Exceptions, and False Positive Investigations",
        "content": "Dependency scanners can report findings that require additional investigation because of vendor backports, package metadata differences, unreachable functionality, or advisory corrections. Teams need a controlled process for evaluating these cases rather than suppressing every inconvenient result. Begin by verifying the package identity, installed version, dependency path, advisory reference, and fixed version. Compare the result with the package vendor's security notice and check whether a distribution has applied a backported patch. If a finding is genuinely not applicable, document the technical evidence, affected component, approving owner, and review date. Use the supported Trivy ignore or policy mechanisms narrowly and avoid broad exclusions that hide unrelated future vulnerabilities. Exceptions should have an expiry or reassessment condition. A scanner suppression is not a remediation: the vulnerable component may still exist in the artifact. Keep the original finding in retained reports so auditors can understand why it was accepted. Reassess exceptions when the package version, application architecture, or security advisory changes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-10",
        "title": "Generating and Reviewing Dependency Inventory Evidence",
        "content": "A dependency inventory helps teams understand which external components are present in an application and supports vulnerability investigation, license review, and software supply chain governance. Trivy can discover package information from supported manifests and lock files, and its reports can provide evidence about package names, versions, and related findings. Engineers should compare the discovered inventory with package manager output and the final build artifact because source metadata and deployed contents may differ. Record the repository commit, build identifier, scanner version, scan timestamp, and target path. For release governance, retain machine-readable reports so security teams can compare component changes across builds. An inventory should not be interpreted as proof that every component is safe; it is a record of identified components that can be evaluated against security intelligence. Review missing or unsupported dependency metadata and ensure that generated dependencies are represented. Reliable inventory evidence improves incident response because teams can quickly identify which applications may contain a newly disclosed vulnerable library."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-4-section-11",
        "title": "Building a Continuous Dependency Risk Management Process",
        "content": "Continuous dependency risk management connects scanning, ownership, remediation, and verification into a repeatable operating process. Start by scanning supported manifests and lock files whenever dependency changes are introduced. Run release-time assessments against the built artifact and periodically reassess deployed versions because new vulnerabilities may be disclosed after publication. Define severity-based response targets and assign each application an accountable owner. Provide developers with clear upgrade guidance and allow controlled exceptions when a fix is unavailable or incompatible. Store scan reports with build and release metadata so findings can be traced to deployed software. Monitor trends such as newly introduced vulnerabilities, overdue remediation, recurring vulnerable packages, and exceptions approaching expiry. Trivy is one part of this process; package manager maintenance, trusted registries, code review, build integrity, and runtime monitoring remain important controls. Review the process regularly to ensure that scanning produces measurable risk reduction rather than simply generating large reports that nobody acts upon."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Container Image Scanning",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-1",
        "title": "Container Image Scanning Fundamentals",
        "content": "Container image scanning evaluates the software components packaged inside a container image and identifies known vulnerabilities and other supported security risks. A container image can include an operating system base, package manager libraries, application dependencies, binaries, and configuration files. Trivy inspects image metadata and filesystem contents to discover installed packages and compare them with vulnerability information. A typical command is trivy image followed by the image reference, such as an application name and tag. Teams should scan the exact image intended for release rather than relying only on a scan of the source repository. Source scanning and image scanning answer different questions: source scanning evaluates repository content, while image scanning examines the assembled artifact. Store the scan report with the image digest, build identifier, scanner version, and scan timestamp. This provides evidence of what was assessed and helps connect security findings to the artifact that will actually be deployed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-2",
        "title": "Understanding Image Layers and Package Discovery",
        "content": "Container images are built from filesystem layers that are combined to form the final filesystem presented to a running container. These layers may originate from a base image, operating system package installation, application dependency installation, and later build instructions. Trivy analyzes image contents to identify operating system packages and application libraries that may contain known vulnerabilities. A package can remain in an image even if a later build instruction removes it from the visible filesystem, depending on how the image was constructed and which layers contain the data. Engineers should understand the difference between the final merged filesystem and the historical image layers. Avoid assuming that deleting a package in a later Dockerfile instruction always removes its data from the image history. Use multi-stage builds, minimal base images, and clean build practices to reduce unnecessary components. Review detected package paths and image metadata when an unexpected vulnerability appears."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-3",
        "title": "Scanning Local Images from Docker and Container Runtimes",
        "content": "A developer or CI agent can scan an image that has already been built and is available through a supported local container runtime. First build the image using the intended Dockerfile and tag it with a recognizable name. Then execute Trivy image against that image reference. The scanner must be able to access the local image through the supported runtime integration or image loading method. If the image cannot be found, confirm the active Docker context, runtime availability, image tag, and permissions of the account running the scan. A common mistake is building an image under one Docker context and scanning from another environment that cannot see it. Record the image ID or digest as well as the human-readable tag because tags can be reassigned. A successful local scan should produce findings for the actual image built by the pipeline, not a similarly named image pulled from an unrelated registry."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-4",
        "title": "Scanning Remote Registry Images and Authentication",
        "content": "Trivy can scan container images stored in supported remote registries, allowing teams to assess artifacts without manually downloading them to a workstation. The scanner needs network connectivity and appropriate registry access. Private registries require authentication, and credentials should be provided through approved environment or credential mechanisms rather than written directly into scripts or source files. Use narrowly scoped credentials that permit reading the required repository and avoid granting unnecessary push or administrative access. If a scan fails with an authentication error, verify the registry hostname, repository path, credential scope, token expiration, and network access. Also confirm that the image tag or digest exists. CI systems should mask secrets in logs and avoid printing authentication configuration. For important release assessments, scan an immutable digest rather than relying only on a mutable tag. This ensures that the report identifies the exact artifact whose security status was evaluated."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-5",
        "title": "Scanning by Image Tag, Digest, and Immutable Artifact Identity",
        "content": "An image tag is a convenient label such as application:release, but tags can be moved to point to different image contents. A digest identifies image content cryptographically and provides a more reliable reference for security evidence. If a pipeline scans a tag and another process replaces that tag before deployment, the deployed artifact may differ from the scanned artifact. Production workflows should capture the digest produced by the build or registry push and use that digest for scanning, promotion, and deployment wherever supported. Store the digest alongside the scan report and release metadata. When investigating a vulnerability, compare the deployed digest with the digest recorded by the scanner. If they differ, the old scan cannot establish the security state of the deployed image. Tags remain useful for human-readable workflows, but they should not be the only artifact identity used in controlled release processes."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-6",
        "title": "Evaluating Base Image Vulnerabilities and Upgrade Decisions",
        "content": "Many container vulnerabilities originate from the operating system or runtime packages included in the base image. A minimal application Dockerfile can still inherit vulnerable packages from a parent image maintained by another team or publisher. Trivy can identify these packages and report relevant vulnerability information, including fixed versions where known. When a base image finding appears, identify the exact base image and distribution release before selecting an upgrade. Review vendor support status, security notices, compatibility, and whether the required patch is available in a newer image. Updating a floating tag without recording its resulting digest can reduce reproducibility. Prefer maintained, trusted base images and rebuild application images regularly so security fixes are incorporated. After changing the base image, rebuild and retest the application, then scan the resulting image again. Do not assume that upgrading an application library will fix a vulnerable operating system package; each package must be remediated at the layer or dependency source that introduced it."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-7",
        "title": "Understanding Application Packages Inside Container Images",
        "content": "Container images frequently contain application libraries in addition to operating system packages. A Java image may include JAR dependencies, a Node.js image may contain npm packages, and a Python image may include installed modules. Trivy can identify supported application package metadata and evaluate discovered versions against vulnerability information. The package location is useful because it helps distinguish application dependencies from operating system components and build tooling. A vulnerability in an application library may require updating the project's dependency declaration and rebuilding the artifact, while an operating system package may require a base image update. Some images contain multiple applications or copied runtime directories, so engineers should review the detected file path and package ecosystem carefully. Compare the scan inventory with the application's lock file and build dependency tree. This helps identify whether a finding was introduced by the application build, the base image, or an additional runtime component."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-8",
        "title": "Container Image Severity Filtering and Release Policy",
        "content": "Image scan reports can contain findings with different severity levels, and teams need a clear policy for deciding which findings block a release. Trivy supports severity filtering so engineers can focus on selected categories during review. Filtering changes which findings are displayed or evaluated; it does not remove vulnerabilities from the image. A production policy should define whether critical and high findings fail a release, whether medium findings require remediation plans, and how accepted exceptions are documented. Consider exploitability, exposure, business criticality, fixed-version availability, and whether the affected component is reachable. Do not rely on severity alone as a complete risk decision. Preserve the full scan report even when the pipeline uses a filtered summary. This allows security teams to investigate lower-severity findings and compare results over time. Keep policy configuration under version control and test it against known sample findings before making it mandatory for every release."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-9",
        "title": "Generating Machine-Readable Container Scan Reports",
        "content": "Machine-readable reports allow CI systems and security platforms to process Trivy findings consistently. JSON output is useful when another pipeline step must inspect vulnerability identifiers, package names, severity, installed versions, and fixed versions. A report can be retained as a build artifact and associated with the commit, image digest, build number, scanner version, and scan timestamp. Human-readable terminal output is still valuable because developers need quick feedback during a build. Keep the full report separate from a short pipeline summary so that truncating console output does not destroy investigation evidence. Downstream processing should handle missing optional fields and scanner output changes carefully. Validate that the report file exists, contains valid JSON, and represents the intended image. A command that exits successfully but produces an empty or unexpected report should not automatically satisfy a release security requirement. Report validation is part of the scanning control."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-10",
        "title": "Integrating Image Scanning into Build and Registry Promotion",
        "content": "A container delivery pipeline should scan an image after it has been built and before it is promoted into a production registry or deployment environment. A typical workflow checks out source code, runs tests, builds the image, captures its immutable digest, performs a Trivy scan, evaluates policy, and then publishes or promotes the artifact if all required controls pass. The exact order depends on whether the image is first pushed to a staging registry or scanned locally. Ensure that the artifact being promoted is the same digest that was assessed. Store scan reports as build evidence and make policy failures understandable to developers. Separate scanner execution errors from findings that violate release policy. A registry promotion process should not silently bypass scanning when the scanner database is unavailable. Provide a documented exception workflow for urgent releases, including approval, expiration, and follow-up remediation. This makes image scanning an enforceable release control rather than an optional report generated after deployment."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-11",
        "title": "Reducing Image Vulnerabilities Through Secure Image Construction",
        "content": "Reducing container image risk begins during image construction rather than after a vulnerability report is produced. Use trusted and maintained base images, install only required operating system packages, avoid unnecessary build tools in runtime images, and remove temporary files using build practices that do not preserve unwanted content in earlier layers. Multi-stage builds can keep compilers and development dependencies out of the final runtime image. Pin important build inputs and record the final digest so the artifact can be reproduced and traced. Keep package managers and application dependencies updated through controlled maintenance. Avoid running applications as root when the application design supports a non-root user, and apply appropriate filesystem permissions. Image minimization does not eliminate vulnerabilities, but it reduces the number of components that require monitoring and patching. Run Trivy after every meaningful image change and periodically reassess retained images because new vulnerabilities may be disclosed after publication."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-5-section-12",
        "title": "Troubleshooting Container Image Scan Failures",
        "content": "Image scanning failures can originate from invalid image references, unavailable registries, authentication problems, runtime access restrictions, network failures, unsupported image formats, or database update problems. Begin by recording the exact command, image tag or digest, scanner version, and full error output. Confirm that the image exists and that the executing account can access it. For private registries, verify credentials and token scope without exposing secrets in logs. If the failure occurs during database initialization, inspect network access, proxy configuration, certificate trust, and cache permissions. If the image is found but package detection is unexpected, check its operating system metadata and filesystem contents. Compare local and remote image references to ensure they point to identical content. Avoid treating a failed scan as a clean result. CI should distinguish a scanner infrastructure failure from a vulnerability policy failure and should prevent release promotion when the required assessment did not complete."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "CVE Database",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-6-section-1",
        "title": "Understanding CVEs and Vulnerability Database Purpose",
        "content": "A Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures identifier, usually called a CVE, is a standardized reference used to identify a publicly disclosed cybersecurity vulnerability. A CVE identifier helps security teams discuss the same issue across scanners, vendors, advisories, and incident reports. Trivy uses vulnerability information from its database ecosystem to evaluate discovered software packages and identify versions associated with known security issues. The database provides information that connects package identity and version ranges with vulnerability records, severity, descriptions, references, and remediation information where available. A scanner cannot identify a vulnerability reliably if it does not have suitable package metadata or relevant intelligence. Database freshness is therefore an important part of scan quality. A scan performed with outdated vulnerability information may miss recently disclosed issues or reflect older advisory details. Teams should understand the difference between the scanner executable and the vulnerability database: updating one does not always mean the other has been refreshed."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-6-section-2",
        "title": "How Trivy Retrieves and Uses Vulnerability Database Data",
        "content": "Trivy uses vulnerability database information during package analysis to determine whether discovered software components match known security advisories. The scanner may retrieve database content from configured distribution sources and use local cache data during execution. The exact database components and update behavior depend on the installed Trivy version and enabled scanning features. When a scan starts, Trivy checks whether the required database information is available and whether an update is needed according to its cache and configuration behavior. It then evaluates detected package names, ecosystems, versions, and distribution details against the available records. Database download failures can prevent a scan from providing current vulnerability results. A successful scan using cached data is not necessarily evidence that the database is current. CI logs should retain database update information and scan metadata. In restricted networks, teams should establish an approved database distribution process instead of silently disabling updates or assuming an old cache is sufficient."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-6-section-3",
        "title": "Vulnerability Advisory Sources and Distribution-Specific Data",
        "content": "Vulnerability information can originate from several advisory publishers, including public vulnerability programs, operating system vendors, language ecosystems, and project maintainers. Trivy combines relevant vulnerability information from supported sources so package findings can be evaluated in context. Distribution-specific advisories are important because Linux vendors sometimes backport security patches into packages without adopting the upstream version number that originally contained the fix. A simple comparison against an upstream version range may therefore produce an incomplete interpretation. Engineers should inspect the operating system distribution, package release, advisory references, and vendor-maintained fixed-version information. When a finding appears inconsistent with a vendor statement, verify whether the installed package includes a backported patch and whether the scanner recognizes the relevant distribution metadata. Do not assume that two advisory sources with similar descriptions are necessarily separate vulnerabilities. Understanding advisory provenance helps security teams avoid incorrect upgrades and make better evidence-based remediation decisions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-6-section-4",
        "title": "Understanding Database Freshness and Scan Reliability",
        "content": "Database freshness describes how recently the vulnerability intelligence used by a scan was updated. Freshness matters because security advisories are published and revised continuously. A database that has not been updated may not contain newly disclosed vulnerabilities or the latest fixed-version information. Trivy can use cached database content, which is useful for performance and restricted environments, but the cache must be managed deliberately. Record the database update time and scanner version with every important report. If a pipeline cannot refresh its database, decide whether policy permits a scan using the existing cache or whether the release must stop. That decision should be visible in the logs. A clean result from stale data must not be presented as equivalent to a clean result from current data. Organizations should define acceptable database age, monitor update failures, and periodically verify that isolated agents receive current database content through an approved distribution process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-6-section-5",
        "title": "Interpreting CVE Records, Affected Versions, and Fixed Versions",
        "content": "A vulnerability record can contain a CVE identifier, description, severity, affected package information, version ranges, references, and fixed-version details. The affected range indicates which package versions are considered vulnerable according to the advisory, while a fixed version identifies a release that includes the relevant correction when one is known. Engineers should verify the package ecosystem and distribution before applying the recommendation because version semantics differ between package managers and vendors. Some advisories describe multiple affected branches or separate fixes for different releases. A package can also be patched by a vendor through a backport, making its version string appear older than an upstream fixed version. Review the original advisory and package publisher guidance before changing production dependencies. Use the fixed version as remediation evidence, then rebuild and rescan the actual application artifact. A scanner finding should be resolved through an updated component or a documented risk decision, not merely by hiding its identifier."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-6-section-6",
        "title": "Database Caching, Storage, and Controlled Distribution",
        "content": "Trivy database caching reduces repeated downloads and helps scans run efficiently across multiple repositories and build jobs. A cache location should be writable by the intended scanner process but protected from untrusted users who could replace database content or disrupt scan execution. Shared CI agents may benefit from a managed cache, while ephemeral agents may need a controlled mechanism to receive current database data. In disconnected environments, administrators can prepare database content through an approved connected system and distribute it to isolated scanners according to supported Trivy procedures. Verify database integrity and compatibility with the installed scanner version. Do not copy arbitrary cache directories between incompatible versions without consulting the documentation. Monitor storage consumption and define safe cleanup behavior so cache maintenance does not remove required data during an active scan. Database distribution should be documented, repeatable, and auditable because vulnerability intelligence is a critical input to security decisions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-6-section-7",
        "title": "Diagnosing Vulnerability Database Download and Update Errors",
        "content": "Database update failures may result from DNS problems, blocked outbound traffic, proxy configuration, TLS certificate trust, registry access, storage permissions, or temporary service outages. Start by checking the exact error and determining whether Trivy failed while downloading database content or while reading an existing cache. Verify that the build agent can reach the approved database source and that proxy environment variables are correct. Check whether a corporate TLS inspection certificate is trusted by the process. Confirm that the cache directory is writable and has sufficient disk space. Avoid deleting the entire cache as the first troubleshooting step because this may increase download time and remove useful evidence. If an update is temporarily unavailable, follow the organization's policy for whether cached data can be used. Record the database timestamp and make the degraded condition visible. Never convert a database update failure into a successful clean security result without an explicit, approved decision."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-6-section-8",
        "title": "Managing Database Behavior in CI and Offline Environments",
        "content": "CI environments should manage vulnerability database behavior as part of the security scanning design. Online agents can update the required database content through approved network paths, while restricted agents may receive prepared database content through a controlled internal distribution mechanism. Define which pipeline stage performs updates and how subsequent scans reuse the resulting cache. Avoid allowing many parallel jobs to trigger unnecessary simultaneous downloads if a managed cache or update stage can serve them safely. Record database timestamps in build artifacts and logs so reviewers can evaluate scan freshness. For offline builds, document the database preparation date, transfer process, scanner compatibility, and maximum permitted age. A disconnected scanner can still identify vulnerabilities represented in its local data, but it cannot know about advisories that have not reached that data. Make this limitation clear in release evidence. Treat database update failure as a separate operational condition from a vulnerability finding."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-6-section-9",
        "title": "Auditing CVE Database Evidence and Continuous Maintenance",
        "content": "Security teams should maintain evidence showing which vulnerability intelligence was used to assess each important software artifact. Retain the Trivy version, database update information, scan timestamp, target commit or image digest, and machine-readable scan report. This allows investigators to reproduce the context of a past security decision and explain why a vulnerability was or was not reported at that time. Database content changes can cause a later scan to report new findings even when the application artifact has not changed. This is expected and demonstrates why periodic rescanning is necessary. Establish ownership for database update monitoring, cache maintenance, and offline distribution. Review failures and stale-data alerts as operational issues rather than ignoring them. Compare scan reports over time to distinguish application changes from vulnerability intelligence changes. A reliable database maintenance process helps organizations respond to new CVEs quickly and prevents outdated security data from creating false confidence in production releases."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Severity Classification",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-7-section-01",
        "title": "Purpose of Vulnerability Severity Classification",
        "content": "Vulnerability severity classification converts raw security findings into a consistent indication of potential risk. A scanner may discover hundreds of CVEs across operating-system packages, language libraries, and application components, but teams cannot treat every finding as equally urgent. Classification helps security engineers, developers, and platform teams understand which issues require immediate containment and which can enter a planned remediation cycle. Severity is an assessment of technical impact and exploit conditions, not a guarantee that an attack has occurred. A production decision should also consider asset importance, exposure, compensating controls, and business impact. Teams should retain the scanner's original severity while documenting any environment-specific prioritization decision separately."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-7-section-02",
        "title": "Understanding the CVSS Scoring Model",
        "content": "The Common Vulnerability Scoring System, or CVSS, provides a standardized way to describe the technical severity of a vulnerability. CVSS versions define metrics that consider factors such as attack vector, attack complexity, privileges required, user interaction, and the effect on confidentiality, integrity, and availability. The resulting score is commonly expressed on a scale from 0.0 to 10.0 and mapped to severity bands. CVSS is useful for comparing findings consistently, but it does not measure the complete business risk of a particular deployment. A vulnerability in an internet-facing authentication service may deserve faster attention than the same vulnerability in an isolated test image, even when both have identical CVSS scores."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-7-section-03",
        "title": "Critical, High, Medium, Low, and Unknown Findings",
        "content": "Critical findings generally represent severe technical conditions that can enable major compromise, such as remote exploitation with broad system impact. High findings can also allow significant compromise and usually need prompt remediation. Medium findings may require particular access, user interaction, or additional conditions, while Low findings commonly have limited impact or difficult exploitation paths. Unknown means the scanner could not assign a reliable severity, perhaps because vulnerability metadata is incomplete or unavailable. Unknown must not automatically be interpreted as safe. Teams should investigate the package identity, advisory source, and available vendor information, then document whether the finding is applicable and what action is required."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-7-section-04",
        "title": "Attack Vector and Exploitability Factors",
        "content": "Attack vector describes how an attacker could reach the vulnerable component, for example through a network, adjacent network, local access, or physical access. Network-reachable weaknesses can be especially concerning when the affected service is publicly exposed. Exploitability also depends on attack complexity, required privileges, and whether a victim must perform an action. These conditions help explain why two vulnerabilities with similar impact may receive different scores. During triage, engineers should verify whether the vulnerable code path is enabled, whether the affected port or endpoint is reachable, and whether authentication or network restrictions reduce exposure. Such controls can change practical priority without changing the published CVSS score."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-7-section-05",
        "title": "Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability Impact",
        "content": "CVSS impact analysis commonly considers confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Confidentiality impact concerns unauthorized access to sensitive information such as credentials, customer records, or internal configuration. Integrity impact concerns unauthorized modification of files, application data, container contents, or system behavior. Availability impact concerns disruption, resource exhaustion, crashes, or denial of service. A finding that can expose secrets and allow code execution may create a different level of concern from one that causes a limited service interruption. In container environments, teams should assess the impact on the container and also on mounted volumes, service accounts, node permissions, and connected production systems."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-7-section-06",
        "title": "Exploit Availability and Threat Intelligence",
        "content": "A published vulnerability score does not always describe how actively a weakness is being used by attackers. Threat intelligence can reveal whether proof-of-concept code exists, whether exploitation has been observed in the wild, or whether the vulnerability appears in authoritative exploited-vulnerability catalogs. This information can raise operational priority, especially for internet-facing workloads. Trivy reports vulnerability metadata from its configured databases, but organizations should verify time-sensitive exploitation information using trusted security advisories and their vulnerability-management process. Record the advisory date, affected package version, exploit status, and evidence source so that urgency decisions can be reviewed rather than relying on an unexplained severity label."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-7-section-07",
        "title": "Environment-Aware Risk Prioritization",
        "content": "Environment-aware prioritization combines scanner severity with deployment context. Consider whether the image runs in production, whether the service is exposed to the internet, what data it handles, and whether the workload has privileged access or sensitive mounted resources. A High finding in a public payment service may require immediate action, while a similar finding in a disposable isolated build environment may be scheduled differently. This does not justify hiding or deleting the original scanner result. Keep severity, contextual risk, owner, due date, and remediation decision as separate fields in reports or tickets. That separation supports auditability and makes risk acceptance visible to the responsible team."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-7-section-08",
        "title": "Trivy Severity Filtering and Pipeline Thresholds",
        "content": "Trivy can filter vulnerability results by severity so a pipeline can focus on findings that match a defined policy. For example, a team may initially report all severities, then use a release gate that fails when Critical or High findings remain. Filtering should be configured deliberately because excluding lower severities from one report can make them less visible to developers. A practical pipeline often stores a complete machine-readable report, publishes a concise human-readable summary, and applies a separately documented failure threshold. Validate the behavior against known test images and review the exact exit-code and severity options for the installed Trivy version before enforcing the policy on production releases."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-7-section-09",
        "title": "Severity Exceptions, Reporting, and Governance",
        "content": "Exceptions should be controlled decisions, not informal instructions to ignore scanner output. A valid exception identifies the affected image or package, the vulnerability identifier, the reason remediation is not currently possible, the compensating controls, an accountable approver, and an expiry or review date. Reports should preserve the scanner's original severity and show the exception status separately. Teams should periodically review expired exceptions, newly available fixed versions, and changes in exploit intelligence. This governance prevents temporary risk acceptance from becoming permanent exposure and provides evidence for audits. Pipeline owners should make the policy understandable to developers by explaining why a build failed and linking the finding to an actionable remediation path."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Misconfiguration Scanning",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-01",
        "title": "What Misconfiguration Scanning Detects",
        "content": "Misconfiguration scanning checks infrastructure and deployment configuration against security rules and recommended practices. Unlike vulnerability scanning, which identifies known weaknesses in software packages, configuration scanning evaluates how resources are defined and operated. Examples include publicly accessible storage, overly permissive network rules, containers running as root, missing resource limits, privileged Kubernetes workloads, and insecure Terraform settings. These conditions may create an attack path even when the software contains no known CVE. Trivy can inspect supported configuration files and identify rule violations before deployment. The purpose is to move security checks earlier into development so teams can correct unsafe defaults before they become live infrastructure."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-02",
        "title": "Supported Configuration Sources and File Discovery",
        "content": "Configuration scanning is useful only when the scanner actually discovers the intended files and understands their formats. A repository may contain Kubernetes YAML, Dockerfiles, Terraform modules, Helm templates, and other infrastructure definitions. Teams should verify which file types and frameworks are supported by their installed Trivy release and whether generated or templated files need to be rendered before scanning. Run scans from a deliberate repository directory and inspect the reported file paths to confirm coverage. Exclusions should be narrow and documented; excluding an entire infrastructure directory can silently remove important security checks. Include representative configuration files in test repositories to validate discovery after scanner upgrades."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-03",
        "title": "Misconfiguration Rules and Security Checks",
        "content": "A misconfiguration rule evaluates a configuration attribute or combination of attributes against a security condition. For example, a rule may detect a container configured with privileged access, a Kubernetes workload that uses the default service account, or an infrastructure resource that permits unrestricted ingress. A finding should explain the affected file and resource, the rule identifier, the reason the configuration is unsafe, and a recommended correction. Rule behavior can evolve as security policies and tool databases change, so teams should record the Trivy version and scan context. Review the rule explanation before changing a resource because a secure correction must preserve the application's intended functionality."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-04",
        "title": "Dockerfile Security Misconfigurations",
        "content": "Dockerfile checks can identify insecure image-building practices that increase runtime risk. Examples include using an unnecessarily privileged user, exposing sensitive build details, selecting outdated or untrusted base images, and omitting controls that reduce the final image attack surface. A safer Dockerfile generally uses a trusted and appropriately maintained base image, installs only required packages, avoids embedding credentials, and switches to a non-root runtime user when the application supports it. Multi-stage builds can separate build dependencies from runtime contents. After changing a Dockerfile, rebuild the image and scan the resulting artifact as well, because the final image may contain inherited configuration or packages that are not obvious from one source file."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-05",
        "title": "Kubernetes Manifest Security Checks",
        "content": "Kubernetes manifest scanning evaluates workload and cluster-resource definitions before they are applied. Important checks include container privilege settings, root execution, host namespace sharing, hostPath mounts, capabilities, read-only root filesystems, service-account usage, and security context configuration. Network policies and resource controls may also be relevant to the overall deployment posture. A manifest that omits a security field may inherit a permissive default depending on the cluster and admission configuration. Teams should review the complete rendered manifest rather than only the source template, then test the corrected resource in a non-production namespace. Scanner findings should be connected to the owning application team and the deployment repository."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-06",
        "title": "Terraform and Infrastructure-as-Code Checks",
        "content": "Infrastructure-as-Code scanning examines declarative resource definitions before cloud infrastructure is created. Common risks include publicly exposed services, unencrypted storage, missing access logging, overly broad identity permissions, and security groups that allow unnecessary inbound traffic. Terraform modules can hide effective settings behind variables, defaults, and module composition, so teams should scan the actual files and understand the evaluated resource behavior. A clean source scan does not replace cloud configuration monitoring because deployed resources can drift or be changed outside the repository. Use version-controlled infrastructure, peer review, plan inspection, and post-deployment checks together to maintain a reliable security control."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-07",
        "title": "Helm Templates and Rendered Manifest Validation",
        "content": "Helm charts generate Kubernetes resources from templates and values files. Scanning only the template source may not reveal every effective setting because values, conditional blocks, and environment-specific overrides influence the rendered output. Render the chart with the same values intended for the target environment, then inspect or scan the resulting manifests where supported. Compare development, staging, and production values because a secure default can be weakened by an override. Keep sensitive values out of rendered artifacts and logs. Validate the rendered output during CI so reviewers can understand the resources that will actually be submitted to the Kubernetes API server."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-08",
        "title": "Understanding Misconfiguration Findings and Evidence",
        "content": "A useful misconfiguration finding must be traceable to a concrete configuration location. Capture the file path, resource name, rule identifier, severity, message, and relevant source lines when the report format provides them. Read the rule explanation and determine whether the flagged configuration is active in the deployment. For example, a manifest may define a privileged setting in a disabled template branch, while a rendered production manifest may not contain it. Conversely, a harmless-looking default can become dangerous when combined with another resource setting. Keep the original report as evidence and record the reasoning behind any accepted exception or false-positive classification."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-09",
        "title": "Severity and Policy for Configuration Findings",
        "content": "Misconfiguration findings should be prioritized according to both the rule severity and the resource context. A public storage resource containing sensitive data or a privileged workload running on a production cluster may require immediate attention. A lower-impact issue in an isolated development environment may be handled through a planned improvement. Define policy thresholds before enabling blocking behavior in CI, and test those thresholds against representative repositories. Do not assume that a scanner's severity label alone captures business risk. Maintain a record of the rule, affected resource, exposure, data sensitivity, owner, and remediation deadline so teams can make consistent decisions across projects."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-10",
        "title": "Custom Policies and Organization-Specific Standards",
        "content": "Organizations often need controls that reflect internal security standards, regulatory obligations, or platform architecture. Trivy supports policy customization capabilities for supported scanning workflows, but the exact policy language and configuration options should be checked against the installed version's documentation. Custom policies can enforce organization-specific requirements such as approved registries, required labels, mandatory resource limits, or restricted workload settings. Keep custom rules in version control, provide examples of compliant and non-compliant configurations, and test rule changes before rollout. A policy should produce a clear explanation and actionable remediation guidance; rules that are ambiguous or overly broad can create alert fatigue and encourage developers to bypass scanning."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-11",
        "title": "Exceptions, Suppressions, and False-Positive Review",
        "content": "Not every reported configuration issue is necessarily exploitable or applicable in a particular deployment. A review may determine that a resource is unused, a template branch is inactive, or a control is enforced by another verified mechanism. Such conclusions should be supported by evidence rather than assumptions. Use the supported exception or suppression mechanism for the installed Trivy version, and scope it to the smallest possible rule and resource. Document the owner, justification, compensating control, approval, and expiry date. Avoid broad exclusions that suppress unrelated findings. Recheck exceptions when the configuration changes, the workload moves to another environment, or the scanner's rule definitions are updated."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-12",
        "title": "CI Pipeline Integration and Quality Gates",
        "content": "A CI pipeline can run configuration scanning when a pull request is opened, when infrastructure files change, and before a release is deployed. A practical workflow checks out the repository, installs or selects a controlled Trivy version, scans the intended configuration paths, saves a machine-readable report, and applies the team's policy threshold. Separate reporting from enforcement during initial adoption so developers can understand existing debt before builds begin failing. Once thresholds are agreed, fail the pipeline for defined high-risk conditions and publish findings as review artifacts. Test both successful and failing examples to ensure that exit codes, report paths, and pipeline behavior are correct."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-13",
        "title": "Remediation Workflow and Secure Configuration Patterns",
        "content": "Remediation should correct the underlying configuration rather than merely hiding the scanner message. Typical improvements include restricting network exposure, applying least-privilege identity permissions, disabling unnecessary container privileges, using non-root execution, protecting secrets, enabling encryption, and defining resource limits. Make changes in the source repository so that the secure setting survives future deployments. Review the proposed change with the application or platform owner, run tests, regenerate rendered manifests when applicable, and scan again to confirm the finding is resolved. Record any remaining risk and avoid changing production infrastructure manually without updating its source of truth."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-8-section-14",
        "title": "Operational Troubleshooting and Continuous Improvement",
        "content": "When a configuration scan returns no findings unexpectedly, first verify the scan command, working directory, input paths, file discovery, scanner version, and enabled scanners. Check whether the repository contains generated files or templates that require rendering. If a finding appears after an upgrade, compare the tool version and rule metadata before assuming the application configuration changed. Keep sample repositories with known insecure and secure examples to detect regressions in scanner behavior. Track recurring finding categories, remediation time, exception age, and policy failures. Use these measurements to improve reusable templates, developer documentation, and platform defaults rather than treating scanning as a one-time release checklist."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Kubernetes Configuration Scanning",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-01",
        "title": "Purpose and Scope of Kubernetes Configuration Scanning",
        "content": "Kubernetes configuration scanning examines cluster resource definitions and workload manifests for insecure settings before those resources are deployed. It helps identify risks such as privileged containers, root execution, host namespace sharing, excessive Linux capabilities, unsafe volume mounts, missing resource controls, and weak security contexts. These checks are important because a workload can use packages without known CVEs and still expose the cluster through an unsafe configuration. Scanning should be performed against source manifests and, where applicable, the final rendered manifests generated by Helm or other templating systems. The goal is to identify risky configuration early, explain the affected resource, and provide a practical correction that preserves application behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-02",
        "title": "Kubernetes Security Contexts and Container Privileges",
        "content": "A Kubernetes securityContext defines security-related settings for a pod or container. Important controls include runAsUser, runAsGroup, runAsNonRoot, allowPrivilegeEscalation, readOnlyRootFilesystem, capabilities, and privileged mode. A container running as root or with unnecessary privileges can increase the impact of an application compromise. For example, a compromised process with excessive capabilities may perform operations that an ordinary application process should not be allowed to perform. Teams should define the least privileges required by the application, test the resulting behavior, and apply settings consistently through reusable workload templates. Scanner findings should be checked against the actual container configuration rather than corrected by blindly copying settings that may break legitimate workloads."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-03",
        "title": "Pod Security Standards and Admission Enforcement",
        "content": "Kubernetes Pod Security Standards describe baseline security expectations for pod workloads through Privileged, Baseline, and Restricted policy levels. The Restricted level applies stronger controls, including restrictions on privilege escalation, host namespaces, capabilities, and other sensitive settings. Pod Security Admission can enforce selected standards at namespace boundaries using enforce, audit, and warn modes. Configuration scanning and admission enforcement serve complementary purposes: scanning detects issues in repository files before deployment, while admission controls evaluate objects submitted to the API server. Teams should understand the policy level assigned to each namespace and test workloads before tightening enforcement. A manifest that passes a static scan should still be validated against the policies and admission controllers configured in the target cluster."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-04",
        "title": "RBAC Roles, RoleBindings, and Service Account Permissions",
        "content": "Role-Based Access Control determines which Kubernetes identities can perform operations against cluster resources. Misconfigured Roles, ClusterRoles, RoleBindings, or ClusterRoleBindings can grant broader access than an application requires. Examples include wildcard permissions, unnecessary cluster-wide privileges, and service accounts that can read secrets or modify workloads without a valid operational reason. Review the verbs, resources, API groups, and binding subjects in each permission definition. Prefer namespace-scoped Roles where cluster-wide access is unnecessary, and create dedicated service accounts for workloads that need API access. Static scanning can identify suspicious policy patterns, but teams should also inspect effective permissions and test access using Kubernetes authorization checks to understand the real privilege boundary."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-05",
        "title": "Network Policies and Workload Network Exposure",
        "content": "Kubernetes NetworkPolicy resources can restrict pod communication when the cluster network plugin supports policy enforcement. Without appropriate network restrictions, workloads may communicate with more services than their function requires. A secure design identifies which applications need inbound connections, which external systems they must reach, and which namespaces are allowed to communicate. Policies should be tested carefully because an incorrect selector or policy rule can block legitimate application traffic. Configuration scanning may identify missing or overly permissive network controls, but the absence of a NetworkPolicy is not automatically proof that a workload is exposed in every cluster. Consider the CNI implementation, existing network controls, service exposure, ingress configuration, and actual traffic requirements when reviewing findings."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-06",
        "title": "Host Namespace Sharing, HostPath, and Sensitive Mounts",
        "content": "Pod settings such as hostNetwork, hostPID, and hostIPC can expose a workload to host-level networking or process and communication namespaces. HostPath volumes can mount directories from a Kubernetes node into a container, potentially exposing sensitive host files or runtime interfaces. These capabilities may be required by carefully controlled infrastructure agents, but they are usually unnecessary for ordinary application workloads. Review every host namespace option and hostPath mount, identify the exact operational requirement, and restrict access to the smallest required path and workload. Prefer safer volume types and narrowly scoped permissions where possible. A scanner finding should be evaluated with the pod's service account, container privileges, node placement, and mount access because these settings can combine to create a much larger security risk."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-07",
        "title": "Secrets, ConfigMaps, and Sensitive Workload Configuration",
        "content": "Kubernetes Secrets are designed to hold sensitive values such as credentials, tokens, and certificates, while ConfigMaps are intended for non-confidential configuration. A Secret object is not automatically safe merely because its data is encoded in Base64; encoding is not encryption. Teams should avoid placing credentials directly in ordinary manifests, source repositories, container images, or ConfigMaps. Restrict Secret access through RBAC, enable appropriate encryption at rest for the cluster's datastore, and consider an external secrets-management integration for sensitive production values. Configuration scanning can help identify suspicious patterns and insecure references, but runtime access controls and secret lifecycle management are also necessary. Rotate credentials if they have been exposed and review repository history rather than only removing the current visible value."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-08",
        "title": "Resource Requests, Limits, and Denial-of-Service Protection",
        "content": "Resource requests and limits help Kubernetes schedule workloads and control their resource consumption. Missing or excessive CPU and memory limits can contribute to noisy-neighbor problems, resource exhaustion, and unstable cluster behavior. A workload that consumes memory without an appropriate limit may affect other pods on the same node, while unrealistic CPU limits can cause throttling and application latency. Define requests based on observed application needs and establish limits that protect shared infrastructure without preventing normal operation. Include ephemeral storage considerations where relevant. Configuration scanning may flag missing resource controls, but values should be validated with performance testing and production metrics. Avoid copying arbitrary resource numbers across services because workload profiles and traffic patterns differ."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-09",
        "title": "Helm Rendering, Kustomize Overlays, and Effective Manifests",
        "content": "Kubernetes configuration often passes through templating and customization tools before deployment. Helm values, template conditionals, Kustomize patches, and environment overlays can change the effective resource configuration. Scanning only one source file may miss a risky setting introduced by an overlay or values file. Render Helm charts with the intended environment values and build Kustomize output before reviewing the final manifests. Validate that the generated output corresponds to the release configuration used by the deployment pipeline. Keep development, staging, and production configuration differences visible in version control. Scan both reusable source definitions and deployment-ready output when appropriate, and investigate any discrepancy between what the scanner inspected and what the cluster actually receives."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-9-section-10",
        "title": "CI/CD Integration, Remediation, and Continuous Validation",
        "content": "Integrate Kubernetes configuration scanning into pull requests and release pipelines so insecure manifests are detected before deployment. A practical workflow checks out the repository, renders templates where necessary, runs the configured scanner against the intended paths, saves a machine-readable report, and applies an agreed policy threshold. During adoption, teams may publish findings without blocking every build, then introduce enforcement for clearly defined high-risk conditions. Each finding should have an owner, explanation, remediation guidance, and tracked status. After changing a manifest, rerun the scan and validate the resource with Kubernetes schema and policy checks. Continue monitoring deployed clusters because manual changes, admission mutations, and configuration drift can cause the live state to differ from the repository."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Secret Scanning",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-01",
        "title": "Purpose and Threat Model of Secret Scanning",
        "content": "Secret scanning searches files and related development artifacts for sensitive values that should not be exposed, including passwords, API tokens, private keys, cloud credentials, database connection strings, and signing secrets. A leaked credential can allow an attacker to access systems directly without exploiting a software vulnerability. Exposure may occur in source code, configuration files, shell scripts, build logs, container layers, documentation, test fixtures, or version-control history. Scanning helps identify likely secrets before code is merged or released, but it cannot guarantee that every credential format or encoded value will be detected. Organizations should combine scanning with secure secret storage, access controls, credential rotation, developer education, and an incident response process for confirmed exposures."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-02",
        "title": "Secret Types, Formats, and Detection Patterns",
        "content": "Sensitive values appear in many formats. Examples include cloud access keys, bearer tokens, database passwords, private key blocks, webhook credentials, service account tokens, and application-specific API keys. Some secrets have recognizable prefixes or fixed structures, while others are arbitrary strings that are difficult to distinguish from ordinary configuration. Secret detection engines use patterns, contextual clues, entropy checks, and provider-specific rules to identify likely exposures. Detection quality depends on the rules and scanner version. Teams should understand which credential types are covered, test representative examples, and avoid assuming that an unreported value is safe. Never place real production credentials in test fixtures merely to verify detection; use synthetic values designed for security testing."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-03",
        "title": "Trivy Secret Scanner and Repository Scanning",
        "content": "Trivy includes secret scanning capabilities for supported input types and can identify potential credentials in files and repositories. A typical workflow runs the scanner against a repository path, reviews the findings, and saves a report for CI or security review. Teams should confirm that secret scanning is enabled and that the command targets the intended files. The scanner's behavior can depend on the installed version, detection rules, file types, and exclusions. Keep the tool version controlled in CI and test it against a small repository containing synthetic secret examples. Review the official documentation for the exact command options supported by the installed release rather than relying on remembered flags from a different version."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-04",
        "title": "Git History, Branches, and Deleted Secret Exposure",
        "content": "Removing a secret from the latest version of a file does not guarantee that it has disappeared from the repository. Git history may retain the original commit, and the value can remain accessible through branches, tags, pull request references, forks, or cached clones. Secret scanning should therefore consider the relevant repository history and not only the current working tree when the selected tool and workflow support historical inspection. If a credential is found in a previous commit, treat it as exposed and rotate or revoke it first. History rewriting may reduce future exposure, but it does not replace credential rotation because other clones and copies may already contain the original value."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-05",
        "title": "False Positives, Test Credentials, and Detection Validation",
        "content": "Secret scanners can report false positives when ordinary strings resemble credential patterns. Examples include sample configuration values, documentation placeholders, hashes, test data, and generated artifacts. Review each finding's file context and determine whether the value is a real credential, a synthetic example, or an unrelated string. Validate scanner behavior using intentionally fake test values and controlled sample repositories. Do not paste a suspected production secret into tickets, chat messages, or public debugging logs while investigating it. If a value cannot be confidently classified, handle it as potentially sensitive until the owner verifies its origin. Record false-positive decisions with a clear reason and keep exceptions narrow so future genuine credentials are not hidden."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-06",
        "title": "Secure Secret Storage and External Secret Managers",
        "content": "Applications should retrieve credentials from approved secret-management systems rather than embedding them in source code or container images. Depending on the environment, teams may use a cloud secret manager, a dedicated vault service, Kubernetes external-secret integration, or a controlled CI/CD credential store. Access should be granted only to the workloads and users that require each value. Prefer short-lived credentials and workload identity where supported, and avoid sharing one powerful credential across unrelated applications. Secret delivery should protect values from appearing in command output, build logs, process arguments, or debugging traces. Document the retrieval method, ownership, rotation process, and recovery procedure so that teams can operate the application without copying secrets into repositories."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-07",
        "title": "CI/CD Pipeline Integration and Pull Request Protection",
        "content": "Secret scanning should run early enough to prevent credentials from entering the main branch or release artifacts. A pipeline can scan changed files during pull request validation and perform broader repository checks on scheduled runs or before a release. When a confirmed secret is detected, the workflow should provide a safe finding summary, identify the affected file and line where available, and block the merge according to the organization's policy. Avoid printing the detected secret value in the CI log. Store machine-readable reports as protected artifacts and limit access to authorized reviewers. Test both passing and failing examples to confirm that pipeline exit codes, report handling, and pull request feedback work as intended."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-08",
        "title": "Credential Rotation and Incident Response",
        "content": "When a real credential is exposed, removing it from the file is not enough. First identify the credential owner, provider, affected systems, permissions, and possible exposure window. Revoke or rotate the credential through the authoritative service, then update dependent applications and deployment systems using the approved secret-delivery mechanism. Review authentication logs and audit events for suspicious activity during the exposure period. Determine whether the credential was copied into Git history, build logs, container images, artifacts, or external systems. Document containment, rotation, verification, and follow-up actions. If a production secret was exposed, follow the organization's incident response process and preserve relevant evidence without spreading the secret further."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-09",
        "title": "Ignore Rules, Exceptions, and Safe Suppression",
        "content": "Some files may legitimately contain synthetic values or content that repeatedly triggers false-positive detections. Use the scanner's supported exclusion or suppression mechanism only after confirming the finding is not a real credential. Keep exceptions specific to the exact file, rule, or test fixture where possible. Avoid excluding broad directories such as the entire source tree, configuration folder, or repository because this can hide future credential leaks. Every exception should have an owner, reason, approval where required, and a review date. Check the installed Trivy documentation for the exact ignore-file syntax and supported behavior. Revisit exceptions when files change, rules are updated, or test data is replaced."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-10-section-10",
        "title": "Reporting, Governance, and Continuous Secret Hygiene",
        "content": "A mature secret-scanning program tracks findings from discovery through verification, rotation, and closure. Reports should identify the repository, affected file, detection rule, finding status, owner, and remediation action without exposing the secret itself. Security teams can measure detection volume, time to rotate exposed credentials, repeat incidents, and the age of unresolved findings. Use these results to improve developer training, reusable templates, pre-commit checks, and secret-management practices. Periodically scan repositories and relevant build artifacts because credentials can be introduced outside normal pull request workflows. Review scanner versions and detection rules as part of tool maintenance, and confirm that reporting and access controls protect the findings as sensitive security information."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "SBOM Generation",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-1",
        "title": "Software Bill of Materials Fundamentals",
        "content": "A Software Bill of Materials, commonly called an SBOM, is a structured inventory of software components included in an application, package, or container image. It can describe direct and transitive dependencies, package versions, component identifiers, suppliers, and relationships between software elements. An SBOM helps engineering and security teams understand what software is present in an artifact instead of relying on assumptions about the source repository. This becomes particularly important when a newly disclosed vulnerability affects a popular library or operating-system package. Teams can use SBOM data to identify potentially affected applications and prioritize investigation. An SBOM is an inventory, not proof that the software is secure; it must be combined with vulnerability analysis, dependency ownership, provenance, and ongoing artifact management."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-2",
        "title": "Why SBOMs Matter in DevSecOps and Supply Chain Security",
        "content": "Modern applications are assembled from many external packages, build tools, base images, and transitive dependencies. A development team may know which libraries it declared directly but may not know every package included in the final production image. SBOM generation makes these components more visible and supports software supply chain transparency. During a security incident, an organization can search its SBOM inventory to determine which services include an affected component. SBOMs also support supplier reviews, software delivery governance, compliance evidence, and vulnerability response. Their value depends on accuracy, freshness, and association with the exact artifact that was deployed. A repository-level inventory should not automatically be treated as identical to the contents of a built container image."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-3",
        "title": "CycloneDX and SPDX SBOM Formats",
        "content": "CycloneDX and SPDX are widely used standards for representing software component inventories. CycloneDX supports component, dependency, service, and vulnerability-related information within its ecosystem. SPDX provides a standardized way to describe software packages, relationships, licensing information, and other supply chain metadata. Both formats can represent useful software inventory information, but their structures and supported fields differ. Teams should select a format based on downstream tools, organizational requirements, and integration compatibility. Trivy supports SBOM generation in supported formats, with exact output options depending on the installed release. Validate generated files against the intended consumer and format version instead of assuming every SBOM parser understands every version or optional field."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-4",
        "title": "Generating SBOMs with Trivy",
        "content": "Trivy can generate an SBOM for supported software artifacts, including container images and filesystem inputs. A typical workflow identifies the exact artifact, invokes the installed Trivy version with its SBOM generation options, selects the required output format, and writes the resulting document to a controlled location. For example, a team can generate an SPDX or CycloneDX report for an application image before publishing that image to a registry. The command must use the correct target and output settings for the installed version. After generation, verify that the file exists, is not empty, parses successfully, and contains expected component information. Keep the scanner version and artifact digest with the report so the SBOM can be traced to the precise build."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-5",
        "title": "Filesystem, Container Image, and Repository SBOM Coverage",
        "content": "SBOM coverage depends on what Trivy scans. A filesystem scan can inventory supported packages and dependencies discovered in a directory, while an image scan can inspect software contained in a container image. These views may differ because the final image can include operating-system packages, copied application artifacts, and dependencies that are not represented in the repository source. A source repository may also contain development dependencies that are excluded from the production image. Teams should decide whether they need an SBOM for source, build output, container image, or multiple stages. For production release tracking, associate the SBOM with the exact image digest rather than relying only on a mutable tag such as latest."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-6",
        "title": "Direct and Transitive Dependency Relationships",
        "content": "Direct dependencies are packages explicitly declared by an application or build configuration. Transitive dependencies are additional packages pulled in by those direct dependencies. A vulnerability in a transitive component can affect an application even when developers never referenced that component directly. SBOM dependency relationships help explain how components are connected and why a package is present. Teams should review dependency metadata, package versions, and relationship information when investigating a security advisory. Some package ecosystems provide richer dependency data than others, so missing relationships should not be interpreted as proof that no dependency exists. Combine SBOM analysis with lockfiles, package-manager metadata, build records, and artifact inspection for a more reliable understanding of the delivered software."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-7",
        "title": "SBOM Metadata, Component Identity, and Accuracy",
        "content": "An SBOM becomes more useful when it identifies the artifact and build context clearly. Important metadata can include the document format and version, generation timestamp, supplier, authoring tool, application name, release version, package identifiers, and artifact references. Component identity should be as precise as the available ecosystem metadata allows. Names alone can be ambiguous because different package registries may contain similarly named projects. Version and package identifiers improve matching against vulnerability and licensing databases. Teams should inspect whether generated records contain expected versions and whether important components are missing. Record the container image digest, source revision, and build identifier alongside the SBOM to support traceability from a deployed workload back to its source and build process."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-8",
        "title": "SBOM Validation, Storage, and Artifact Association",
        "content": "Generating a report is only the first step. Validate the SBOM structure with a compatible parser or validator and confirm that the document can be consumed by downstream inventory systems. Check component counts, package names, versions, relationships, and metadata for obvious gaps. Store the SBOM as a release artifact with access controls and retention appropriate to the software lifecycle. Associate it with the exact build, source revision, image digest, and release identifier. Avoid overwriting an older SBOM with a new document that uses the same generic filename because historical inventory is important during incident investigations. A release pipeline should fail or alert when SBOM generation produces an empty, invalid, or unexpectedly incomplete report."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-9",
        "title": "CI/CD SBOM Automation and Release Integration",
        "content": "A CI/CD pipeline can generate an SBOM after building an application or container image and before promoting the artifact to a production registry. A practical sequence checks out source code, builds the artifact, scans the final artifact, generates the SBOM, validates the output, and publishes both the artifact and its inventory. The pipeline should capture the tool version, format, output location, and artifact digest. Store the SBOM alongside the release metadata so deployment systems and security teams can retrieve it later. If generation fails, define whether the release must stop or whether the issue should create an explicit warning. Do not silently skip SBOM generation because a missing inventory reduces the organization's ability to respond to supply chain vulnerabilities."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-11-section-10",
        "title": "SBOM Lifecycle, Vulnerability Response, and Governance",
        "content": "An SBOM should remain associated with a software artifact throughout its lifecycle. When a new vulnerability is announced, security teams can search stored inventories to identify releases containing the affected component and notify the relevant owners. When an application is rebuilt, its new SBOM should reflect the components actually included in the new artifact. Retain historical inventories so teams can investigate older releases and determine whether a vulnerable component was present at a particular time. Define ownership for generation, validation, storage, access, and retirement of SBOM records. Measure coverage across production artifacts, freshness of inventories, and time required to identify affected services. These practices turn SBOM generation from a one-time compliance activity into a practical software supply chain control."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "topicTitle": "Ignore Policies",
    "sections": [
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-1",
        "title": "Purpose and Scope of Trivy Ignore Policies",
        "content": "Ignore policies control how selected scanner findings are treated during a scan or report review. They can help teams manage confirmed false positives, documented risk exceptions, accepted findings, or temporary conditions that cannot be fixed immediately. An ignore decision should never be treated as proof that a vulnerability does not exist. The original finding may still represent a real weakness, even when the organization decides not to block a particular build. Trivy supports ignore and suppression capabilities whose exact configuration and syntax depend on the installed version and scan type. Teams should understand the behavior of each mechanism and preserve visibility into ignored findings so security debt does not disappear from reports without accountability."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-2",
        "title": "False Positives Versus Accepted Security Risk",
        "content": "A false positive occurs when a scanner reports an issue that does not actually apply to the inspected component or configuration. An accepted risk is different: the finding is considered real or potentially real, but the organization has approved a decision to tolerate it under documented conditions. For example, a package may be affected by a vulnerability but the vulnerable code path may not be reachable in a particular deployment. That requires evidence and risk review; it does not automatically make the scanner result false. Keep these classifications separate in reports and tickets. A false-positive decision should explain why the rule does not apply, while risk acceptance should document business justification, compensating controls, ownership, approval, and review date."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-3",
        "title": "Understanding .trivyignore and Supported Ignore Syntax",
        "content": "Trivy provides an ignore-file mechanism for supported findings, commonly using a .trivyignore file in the scan context. It can be used to identify selected vulnerability identifiers that should be excluded from normal reporting or enforcement behavior. The exact syntax, supported fields, and advanced capabilities can vary between Trivy versions and scanning modes. Teams should consult the documentation for the installed release before adding identifiers or metadata to an ignore file. Keep the file under version control so changes can be reviewed alongside application code. Do not assume that every scanner category uses identical ignore behavior, and test a controlled example to confirm the policy has the intended effect before applying it to production pipelines."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-4",
        "title": "Scoped Exceptions and Minimum Necessary Suppression",
        "content": "An effective ignore policy should suppress only the finding that has been reviewed and approved. Broad exclusions can hide unrelated vulnerabilities and make future scan results misleading. Prefer narrow identifiers, specific package context, and clearly scoped exceptions supported by the installed Trivy version. Where a policy mechanism supports package or path conditions, verify that the condition matches only the intended target. Review how the scanner handles the same vulnerability identifier when it appears in multiple packages or artifacts. After adding an exception, run the scan against both the intended finding and a separate known finding to confirm that the exception has not hidden unrelated issues. The principle is to minimize suppression while retaining enough context for reviewers to understand the decision."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-5",
        "title": "Expiry Dates, Owners, and Exception Lifecycle",
        "content": "An ignore entry should have a lifecycle rather than remaining in a repository forever. Assign an accountable owner, record the reason for the exception, and establish a review or expiry date. A temporary exception may be justified while a vendor prepares a patch or while a controlled migration is underway, but it should be reconsidered when a fixed version becomes available. Teams should periodically inspect ignored identifiers, determine whether the original condition still exists, and remove exceptions that are no longer necessary. If the installed Trivy version supports exception metadata such as expiry or explanatory comments, use those features according to the documented syntax. Otherwise, maintain a linked exception register or ticket so that ownership and review obligations remain visible."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-6",
        "title": "Severity-Based Policies and Build Gate Behavior",
        "content": "Ignore policies and severity thresholds solve different problems. A severity threshold determines which classes of findings should affect a pipeline decision, while an ignore policy changes the treatment of specific reviewed findings. A team may configure a release gate to fail on Critical and High vulnerabilities while allowing lower-severity findings to be reported for remediation. A narrow approved exception may then apply to a particular identifier under documented conditions. Preserve complete reports so filtering or suppression does not remove all visibility. Test the combined behavior using representative images and known findings. Confirm the actual exit codes and report contents for the installed Trivy version, because pipeline behavior should be based on observed results rather than assumptions about configuration."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-7",
        "title": "CI/CD Governance and Pull Request Review",
        "content": "Ignore-policy changes should be reviewed like other security-sensitive code changes. A pull request that adds an exception should explain the affected identifier, package or resource, technical justification, risk owner, compensating controls, and review deadline. Security reviewers should verify the evidence and confirm that the exception is no broader than necessary. The CI pipeline should retain the original scan report and show which findings were ignored or excluded where the selected report and configuration support that distinction. Protect policy files with code ownership or approval rules when appropriate. This process prevents developers from bypassing release controls simply to make a pipeline green and makes risk decisions traceable across application teams and security governance."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-8",
        "title": "Ignore Policies for Vulnerabilities, Misconfigurations, and Secrets",
        "content": "Different Trivy scanning categories can have different finding identifiers, policy mechanisms, and remediation requirements. Vulnerability exceptions may refer to CVE identifiers, while misconfiguration findings may use rule identifiers and secret findings may identify detected credential patterns. A policy designed for one category should not be assumed to control another category automatically. Teams should verify which ignore mechanisms are supported for the specific scanner and report type being used. Be especially cautious with secret findings: suppressing a detected credential does not revoke or secure the credential itself. A confirmed leaked secret should be rotated or revoked through the relevant provider. Maintain separate review criteria for vulnerabilities, infrastructure configuration weaknesses, and exposed credentials."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-9",
        "title": "Auditing, Reporting, and Visibility of Ignored Findings",
        "content": "Security reporting should make ignored findings visible rather than presenting a misleading picture of a completely clean scan. Retain the original scanner output where practical and record the identifier, package or resource, decision type, owner, justification, and expiry or review date. Track how many findings are ignored, how long exceptions remain active, and which teams repeatedly request similar suppressions. A sudden increase in ignored findings may indicate that policy thresholds are too strict, remediation support is insufficient, or teams are bypassing controls. Periodic audits should compare the exception register with current scan results and confirm that each decision is still valid. This provides evidence for internal reviews and helps prevent exception files from becoming unmanaged security debt."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-10",
        "title": "Troubleshooting Ignore Policy Behavior",
        "content": "When an expected finding is still reported after an ignore entry is added, check the working directory, ignore-file path, identifier spelling, file formatting, and the installed Trivy version. Confirm that the scanner is using the intended configuration and that the finding belongs to the category controlled by that ignore mechanism. If a finding disappears unexpectedly, verify that a broad exclusion has not hidden other packages or resources. Compare a full scan report with the pipeline summary to distinguish filtering from actual remediation. Test policy changes against known vulnerable and non-vulnerable examples. Record the exact command and tool version when troubleshooting so that another engineer can reproduce the behavior without relying on undocumented assumptions."
      },
      {
        "id": "stage-7-topic-12-section-11",
        "title": "Production Exception Review and Continuous Policy Improvement",
        "content": "Production ignore policies should be reviewed continuously as application versions, vulnerability intelligence, scanner rules, and business exposure change. A finding that was accepted in a development environment may require a different decision when the same artifact is promoted to an internet-facing production service. Reassess exceptions when a fixed package version becomes available, a workload changes its exposure, or a new exploit is reported. Remove expired entries and verify that previously ignored findings reappear when the exception is removed. Use exception trends to improve base images, dependency management, secure templates, and remediation guidance. A mature policy makes exceptions possible when justified while ensuring that every decision remains limited, documented, accountable, and reversible."
      }
    ]
  },
